1. How Spring Boot Works

When a frontend/mobile app sends request:

Client → Controller → Service → Repository → Database

Example:

POST /expenses

Flow:

ExpenseController
    ↓
ExpenseService
    ↓
ExpenseRepository
    ↓
MySQL Database


Important Dependecies

1. Spring Web
Purpose

Used to build REST APIs and web applications.

What it provides
HTTP request handling (GET, POST, etc.)
REST controllers
JSON conversion
Embedded server like Tomcat
If your app exposes APIs or web endpoints, you need this.

2. Spring Data JPA
Used to interact with databases using Java objects instead of writing lots of SQL manually.

What it provides
ORM support (via Hibernate)
Repository pattern
CRUD operations
Automatic query generation

Makes database operations much easier and cleaner.

3. MySQL Connector/J
Purpose

Allows your Java application to connect to a MySQL database.

What it provides
JDBC driver implementation
Communication between Spring Boot and MySQL
Database connectivity.

4. Project Lombok
Reduces boilerplate Java code.
What it provides

Automatically generates:

getters/setters
constructors
toString()
equals() and hashCode()

Makes code shorter and cleaner.


Annotations
1. Core Spring Boot Annotations
@SpringBootApplication

Main entry point of a Spring Boot app.

@SpringBootApplication
public class App {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(App.class, args);
    }
}

It combines:

@Configuration
@EnableAutoConfiguration
@ComponentScan
@Configuration

Marks a class as a configuration class.

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
}
@Bean

Creates and manages an object as a Spring Bean.

@Bean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
}
2. Dependency Injection Annotations
@Component

Generic Spring-managed bean.

@Component
public class MyComponent {
}
@Service

Used for service/business logic classes.

@Service
public class UserService {
}
@Repository

Used for DAO/repository classes.

@Repository
public class UserDao {
}
@Controller

Used in MVC web applications.

@Controller
public class HomeController {
}
@RestController

Used for REST APIs.

@RestController
public class ApiController {
}

Equivalent to:

@Controller + @ResponseBody
@Autowired

Injects dependencies automatically.

@Autowired
private UserService service;

Constructor injection is preferred nowadays.

3. REST API Annotations
@RequestMapping

Maps URLs.

@RequestMapping("/users")
@GetMapping

Handles GET requests.

@GetMapping("/users")
@PostMapping

Handles POST requests.

@PostMapping("/users")
@PutMapping

Handles PUT requests.

@PutMapping("/users/{id}")
@DeleteMapping

Handles DELETE requests.

@DeleteMapping("/users/{id}")
@PathVariable

Reads values from URL path.

@GetMapping("/users/{id}")
public User get(@PathVariable Long id)
@RequestParam

Reads query parameters.

@GetMapping("/search")
public String search(@RequestParam String name)
@RequestBody

Reads JSON request body.

@PostMapping
public void save(@RequestBody User user)
@ResponseBody

Converts return value into JSON/text response.

4. Spring Data JPA Annotations
@Entity

Marks a class as a database entity.

@Entity
public class User {
}
@Table

Specifies table name.

@Table(name = "users")
@Id

Primary key field.

@Id
private Long id;
@GeneratedValue

Auto-generates IDs.

@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
@Column

Customizes column mapping.

@Column(name = "user_name")
@Transient

Ignores field from DB mapping.

@Transient
private String temp;
